Hi to all,

I looked at this example about ridge regression and
tried to understand what exactly happens here. It's not completely
documented, but maybe I am already right with my strategy.

To describe my problem:

The
 basic model is to describe the expression activity of two alleles by:
<expression level> = <activity allele 1>*<count of allele
 1> + <activity allele 2>*<count of allele 2>  ; no
intercept!!!!

Now I would like to see whether a second gene influences the expression level. For this purpose, I add this ridge term:
<expression
 level> ~ <count of allele
 1> + <count of allele 2>  + f(beta1, <count of influencing
allele 1>, model="iid", values=c(1,2)) + f(beta2, <count of
influencing allele 2>, copy="beta1")
... compare the example ...

But what happens now? What am I specifying with the <count of influencing allele 1> etc.? A weight vector?
And
 I do something else:  <count of influencing allele 1> + <count
 of influencing allele 2> := constant positive, and the coefficients
are not necessarily pairwise positive and negative ... so my question
is: do I have some intrinsic intercept, that the "iid" term does not is
balanced to 0?

I tried something weird, to include an intercept
term as f(beta3, <intercept>, copy="beta1"), with
<intercept> representing an "always 1" vector ... but it seems not
 to be necessary, as it is about 0+/-1e-9?

Can you help me understanding this R-INLA function?

Best,
Andreas

From u673086e375eccf25 at groups.google.com  Fri Jan 11 14:29:59 2013
From: u673086e375eccf25 at groups.google.com (Andreas Recke)
Date: Fri, 11 Jan 2013 14:29:59 +0000
Subject: Re: Ridge regression
Message-ID: <W1N4qNog_WU.V9tqy-455KcJ@r-inla-discussion-group.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <W1N4qNog_WU.Phsn8yAtzgYJ@r-inla-discussion-group.groups.google.com>
References: <W1N4qNog_WU.Phsn8yAtzgYJ@r-inla-discussion-group.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/r-inla-discussion-group/c/W1N4qNog_WU/m/V9tqy-455KcJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

 <https://lh6.googleusercontent.com/-i6AYidChNnk/UPAh484qfLI/AAAAAAAAAAU/lDXt-MzcTN4/s1600/Samplefig.png>
Hi,

to describe the math of the model better:

y: vector of gene expression level
x1, x2: vector of counts of allels for genes (in the regular case: x1+x2:=2)

To estimate the amount of gene expression produced by x1 and x2, the model y ~ x1 + x2 + 0 is to be solved.

Now
 I would like to see whether some additional information of influencing
genes can be included.I can see that another gene (w with alleles w1 and
 w2, but w1+w2 is not constant 2 ) leads to a nearly linear increase of y
 (compare attached figure), even if the mRNA of this gene is not
detected (!!!), so I would change the model to:

y ~ x1 + x2 + 0  + [w1 + w2 + an_intercept_to_balance_influence_of_gene_w]

the
 term in squared brackets should be centered around 0. It might be, that
 it is not centered around 0, but if there is no influence of Gene w,
then this term should not lead to a model like this:

y ~ x1 + x2 + 1 ... because x1 and x2 are both 100% inversely correlated, an intercept in this model would lead to bad results.

What
 I now tried was the following idea: w1, w2 and this intercept for w all
 come from a common distribution N(0, sigma^2). This is expressed by the
 ridge term ... by this, sigma^2 will be as small as possible, if
there
 is no explanatory value in gene w. The principle behind ridging - as
far as I understood it here - is to assume that all coefficients are
small.

So: the model worked, but I was asking myself whether it
is necessary to formulate it for INLA together with this intercept. The
alternatives I see are:

f(beta1, w1, model="iid", values=c(1,2,3)) + f(beta2, w2, copy="beta1") + f(beta3, intercept_w, copy="beta1")

or

f(beta1, w1, model="iid", values=c(1,2)) + f(beta2, w2, copy="beta1")    no intercept

The
 result was, with both approaches, that the coeffients for x1 and x2
stayed the same, but I found a relevant coeffienct for w1, while w2 and
intercept were nearly 0.

****
Then I became more tough and
tried to combine more ridged terms to determine whether different
"aspects" of gene w are important.

I have a copy numbered gene,
so w1 + w2 = sum(w) ; w1 and w2 describe here alleles which differ at a
SNP at position A.  w1_B and w2_B do the same, but describe SNP at
position B.
w1_B is not equal to w1, but w1 + w2 = w1_B + w2_b = ...

Now I added the following multiple ridging terms:

f(beta1, w1, model="iid", values=c(1,2,3)) + f(beta2, w2, copy="beta1") + f(beta3, intercept_w, copy="beta1")  +
  f(alpha1, w1_B, model="iid", values=c(1,2,3)) + f(alpha2, w2_B, copy="alpha1") + f(alpha3, intercept_w_B, copy="alpha1")

I
 can calculate the model for all ridging terms separately, and they are
staying nearly the same, which speaks for the stability of this
approach. In addition, I see that the alpha model above leads to very
small variance
for the iid model, while for beta it is much bigger. I am not sure that this means the alpha term is not as important as the beta term.

***

